WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.479
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.779 --> 00:00:17.225
Unit forty-seven: Psychology of Persuasion. In this unit, you will analyse the psychological principles behind persuasion.

4
00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:19.520
social proof.

5
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.924
social proof means doing something because many others do it.

6
00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:27.630
'Best-seller' labels use social proof.

7
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.911
Your turn. Say: social proof.

8
00:00:34.611 --> 00:00:35.787
social proof.

9
00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:38.028
reciprocity.

10
00:00:38.728 --> 00:00:41.900
reciprocity means the urge to return favours.

11
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.140
Free samples work through reciprocity.

12
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.911
Your turn. Say: reciprocity.

13
00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:52.739
reciprocity.

14
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.914
commitment bias.

15
00:00:55.614 --> 00:00:59.807
commitment bias means the tendency to stick with a choice once we've made it.

16
00:01:00.107 --> 00:01:02.908
Commitment bias keeps people in bad projects.

17
00:01:03.608 --> 00:01:06.458
Your turn. Say: commitment bias.

18
00:01:09.158 --> 00:01:10.461
commitment bias.

19
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:12.834
authority bias.

20
00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:18.854
authority bias means trusting someone too much because they seem to be an expert.

21
00:01:19.154 --> 00:01:22.013
Adverts with doctors use authority bias.

22
00:01:22.713 --> 00:01:25.377
Your turn. Say: authority bias.

23
00:01:28.077 --> 00:01:29.299
authority bias.

24
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:31.830
scarcity principle.

25
00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:37.130
scarcity principle means valuing things more when they seem rare.

26
00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.919
'Only three left!' uses the scarcity principle.

27
00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:44.909
Your turn. Say: scarcity principle.

28
00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:49.110
scarcity principle.

29
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:51.501
liking principle.

30
00:01:52.201 --> 00:01:56.940
liking principle means being persuaded more easily by people we like.

31
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.006
Friendly salespeople use the liking principle.

32
00:02:00.706 --> 00:02:04.171
Your turn. Say: liking principle.

33
00:02:06.871 --> 00:02:08.221
liking principle.

34
00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:10.485
foot-in-the-door.

35
00:02:11.185 --> 00:02:15.982
foot-in-the-door means getting a small yes first, then asking for more.

36
00:02:16.282 --> 00:02:18.688
The survey used a foot-in-the-door technique.

37
00:02:19.388 --> 00:02:22.865
Your turn. Say: foot-in-the-door.

38
00:02:25.565 --> 00:02:26.682
foot-in-the-door.

39
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:28.986
door-in-the-face.

40
00:02:29.686 --> 00:02:35.064
door-in-the-face means asking for something big first, then a smaller request.

41
00:02:35.364 --> 00:02:38.618
He used door-in-the-face to get a discount.

42
00:02:39.318 --> 00:02:43.178
Your turn. Say: door-in-the-face.

43
00:02:45.878 --> 00:02:47.564
door-in-the-face.

44
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.712
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:51.012 --> 00:02:54.800
Rithy, why do you think you bought that course after the free webinar?

46
00:02:56.083 --> 00:02:59.978
It must have been reciprocity. They gave me something for free first.

47
00:03:01.283 --> 00:03:03.678
And the countdown timer on the page?

48
00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:08.379
That might have triggered the scarcity principle. I panicked!

49
00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:11.660
Did they show testimonials?

50
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.718
Lots. That would have been social proof.

51
00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:22.116
These techniques can't be called dishonest in themselves, but they can be misused.

52
00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:27.680
Next time, I should wait twenty-four hours before buying anything.

53
00:03:28.983 --> 00:03:32.225
Grammar focus: Modals of deduction (mastery).

54
00:03:32.525 --> 00:03:35.186
Use modals to analyse why something happened.

55
00:03:35.486 --> 00:03:39.613
It must have been reciprocity. The timer might have triggered scarcity.

56
00:03:39.913 --> 00:03:41.866
That would have been social proof.

57
00:03:42.166 --> 00:03:48.241
'Would have been' can express a confident logical assumption about the past, not only a conditional.

58
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.866
Now read and listen. The Hidden Forces of Influence.

59
00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:59.728
Psychologist Robert Cialdini identified several principles that make people say yes.

60
00:04:01.017 --> 00:04:05.605
Reciprocity explains why free samples often lead to purchases.

61
00:04:06.917 --> 00:04:11.714
Social proof makes us follow the crowd, especially when we feel uncertain.

62
00:04:13.017 --> 00:04:17.327
Authority bias leads us to trust people in uniforms or white coats.

63
00:04:18.617 --> 00:04:22.439
The scarcity principle makes limited offers seem more valuable.

64
00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:30.022
Commitment bias means that once we agree to something small, we are more likely to agree to more.

65
00:04:31.317 --> 00:04:36.068
Marketers, politicians and even charities use these principles every day.

66
00:04:37.383 --> 00:04:41.066
Understanding them may be the best defence against manipulation.

67
00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.235
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:45.535 --> 00:04:47.917
The urge to return favours is blank.

69
00:04:48.217 --> 00:04:53.624
A, reciprocity. B, authority bias. C, scarcity.

70
00:04:57.617 --> 00:05:00.336
The answer is A: reciprocity.

71
00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.398
He's late. He blank missed the bus.

72
00:05:04.698 --> 00:05:08.525
A, must have. B, can't have. C, shouldn't.

73
00:05:12.517 --> 00:05:15.433
The answer is A: must have.

74
00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:21.991
She blank seen the advert; it was only on TV, and she has no TV.

75
00:05:22.291 --> 00:05:26.421
A, must have. B, can't have. C, would have.

76
00:05:30.417 --> 00:05:33.426
The answer is B: can't have.

77
00:05:34.917 --> 00:05:37.453
'Only three left!' uses the blank.

78
00:05:37.753 --> 00:05:44.239
A, scarcity principle. B, liking principle. C, foot-in-the-door.

79
00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:51.747
The answer is A: scarcity principle.

80
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:57.537
According to the text, what may be the best defence against manipulation?

81
00:05:57.837 --> 00:06:03.951
A, avoiding shops. B, understanding these principles. C, trusting experts.

82
00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:11.424
The answer is B: understanding these principles.

83
00:06:12.917 --> 00:06:19.458
Now it's your turn. Part one: Describe a time you were persuaded to buy or do something.

84
00:06:24.758 --> 00:06:30.264
Part two: Analyse it with modals of deduction and name two principles involved.

85
00:06:36.564 --> 00:06:49.958
Last month, I bought expensive headphones. It must have been social proof: thousands of five-star reviews. The 'sale ends tonight' banner might also have triggered the scarcity principle.

86
00:06:50.258 --> 00:06:52.885
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:53.883 --> 00:06:59.496
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modals of deduction (mastery).

88
00:06:59.796 --> 00:07:03.598
Today's value is good habits. Do good things every day.

89
00:07:03.898 --> 00:07:06.597
See you in Unit forty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:06.897 --> 00:07:09.479
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

